**`GlobalExceptionHandler.cs`** — the single class that handles everything:

```csharp
public sealed class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext, Exception exception, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException      => (404, "Resource Not Found"),
            ArgumentException         => (400, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (409, "Conflict"),
            _                         => (500, "Internal Server Error")
        };

        var problemDetails = new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status   = statusCode,
            Title    = title,
            Detail   = statusCode < 500 ? exception.Message
                                        : "An unexpected error occurred.",
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        };

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, ct);
        return true;
    }
}
```

**`Program.cs`** — two lines of registration, no per-endpoint try-catch:

```csharp
// Registration
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();

// Middleware (before routing)
app.UseExceptionHandler();
```

### How it works

1. **`AddProblemDetails()`** — configures the framework to produce RFC 7807 JSON for errors.
2. **`AddExceptionHandler<T>()`** — registers your `IExceptionHandler` in DI.
3. **`UseExceptionHandler()`** — adds middleware that catches any unhandled exception and routes it to your handler.

The pattern-match maps exception types → status codes. For 5xx, the `Detail` field is generic so no stack traces or internals leak. For 4xx client errors, the exception message is surfaced since it's expected to be user-facing.

The full files are in `ProblemDetailsDemo/` if you want to run them.
